\mt1 EDITOR’S INTRODUCTION
\ip The Old Testament is eBible.org’s transcription of Sir Lancelot Charles Lee Brenton’s English Septuagint. It follows the 1870 text, with the corrections that eBible has made to it. Brenton’s preface, introduction, introduction to the Apocrypha, abbreviations, notes, and appendices are all kept.
\ip The New Testament is the twenty-seven books of eBible.org’s KJV Cambridge Paragraph Bible, with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. The historical Cambridge title page has been left out, and Scrivener’s critical introduction and scholarly appendices, which are not part of the digital package, do not appear here. Both sources describe their texts as public domain; eBible’s KJV notice also records the Crown’s continuing printing rights in the United Kingdom.
\ip The volume is in two parts, and each opens with the front matter of its own translation. The Old Testament begins with Brenton’s preface of 1844, his introduction of 1870, his introduction to the Apocrypha, and his list of the abbreviations and signs used in the notes. The New Testament begins with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. Closing notes stay with the books they belong to. After Revelation come three appendices: Brenton’s table of chapters and verses in Jeremias, his notes and supplied passages, and eBible’s corrections to the text.
\ip The books are arranged in the Church of Greece’s Old Testament order, with the Prayer of Manasses after 2 Chronicles and 4 Maccabees after 3 Maccabees. Brenton’s source labels its combined book “Ezra and Nehemiah”; here it is divided after chapter 10 into 2 Esdras and Nehemias 1–13, and the separate source file labelled “Nehemiah,” which duplicates that material, is omitted. The additions to Esther, the Song of the Three Children in Daniel 3, and Psalm 151 are kept. Susanna comes before Daniel 1 and Bel and the Dragon after Daniel 12, both as parts of Daniel; the running heads number them Daniel 0 and Daniel 13. Susanna, the Song of the Three Children, and Bel and the Dragon each have a heading added by this edition. Malachias 3:19–24 is numbered 4:1–6, and the cross-reference note at 3:23 is labelled 4:5 to match.
\ip The wording and notes of the sources have not been changed. Added words are set in regular type. Apart from the Ezra–Nehemias, Daniel, and Malachias labels described above, the chapter and verse labels are those of the sources. The book order, title pages, section dividers, and table of contents are this edition’s own. So are several book titles: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi; his Jezekiel is Ezekiel and Michæas is Michaias; his Kings are 1–4 Kingdoms, his Song of Solomon is the Song of Songs, and his Lamentations is the Lamentations of Jeremias; numbered books put the number first; “(Greek)” is dropped from Esther and Daniel and “(Ecclesiasticus)” from Sirach; the Gospel, Pauline, and Catholic Epistle titles call their named saints “Saint”; and titles and headings lose their closing full stops. The seven Catholic Epistles take their group name from the Greek tradition, including the second and third epistles of John. A few headings in the front matter and appendices have also been reworded. The opinions expressed in the introductions and appendices are those of their original authors.
\ip The volume was typeset with PTXprint 3.0.43 in its BSB A5 layout. The text is set in Utopia, with GFS Porson for Greek and Ezra SIL for Hebrew. This is an English Bible; Greek and Hebrew appear only in the introductions, notes, and appendices.
